# Line endings are part of the reproducibility claim.
#
# README.md states that src/hydrolex_bench.py is byte-identical to the script
# that produced the published results. Without the rules below, Git's default
# behaviour on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) checks that file out with CRLF, so
# a clone on Windows and a clone on Linux would differ byte for byte and any
# checksum of the frozen script would depend on the operating system.
#
# Forcing LF in the working copy keeps the script, the JSON result files and
# the documentation identical on every platform. Python, NumPy and SciPy read
# LF files correctly on Windows, so nothing here affects how the benchmark runs.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.cff   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

*.png   binary
